  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nA small shortwave ridge is briefly overhead early this morning.\nThis ridge gets replaced by a sharp upper trof, now in far\nwestern Ontario. This trof is taking on a more neutral to slight\nnegative tilt, as an individual wave rounds its base. An\nassociated surface low is well north of Lk Superior, moving\nese-ward. It passes well to our north today, dragging an\ncold/occluded front across northern MI this morning and early\nafternoon.\n\nPresently quiet in our neighborhood, though banded cirrus is\nincreasing just to our west. Some fog has formed in vicinity of\nthe Soo (KANJ/CYAM have seen vsbys down to 1/4SM at times), but\nthat's it. A tightening pressure gradient and associated \nincreasing low-level winds should erode the fog this morning. \nThicker/ layered cloud is just north of Lk Superior and MN. \nBetter moisture with this system looks to be somewhat behind the\nfront, and current sat imagery is in agreement.\n\nThe other process of note is increasing overlake instability\nbehind the boundary. 850mb temps drop to slightly below 0C by\nlate afternoon. Expect lake enhanced showers in eastern upper MI\nto get going toward midday, and in n central and ne lower MI in\nthe afternoon. 1000-850mb winds will be slowly/steadily veering,\nfrom wnw at midday to nnw by midnight. Sct to numerous showers\nexpected, except for the far sw. The BL will be warm enough to\nkeep precip liquid into tonight. Pops diminish toward/after\nmidnight as moisture band exits east. Brisk to windy, especially\nthis afternoon/evening, with some 30-35mph gusts in exposed\nlocales. \n\nA somewhat more prominent shortwave ridge slides in from the wnw\non Monday. This contributes to quieter wx early in the work\nweek. A clipper-type digging shortwave passes a little to our\nnorth Monday night/Tue, but better forcing/moisture is still\nstaying mostly to our north. Some small pops for now, mainly in\nparts of Chippewa Co. Midweek is likely to feature cyclogenesis\nin the central/eastern CONUS. Though some models may overstate \nthe degree to which this system interact/coalesces/phases, some \nsort of strengthening system is likely to cross the OH Valley or\nGreat Lakes area. Plenty of solutions have a storm track too \nfar south for significant QPF here, which is limiting pops for \nnow. Eventually, sharply colder air gets dragged southward \nbehind this system. However, for more the northerly fetch limits\nthe impacted areas of expected lake effect precip.\n\nMax temps somewhat milder today into Tue, with Tuesday still\nlooking the warmest. Wednesday is a transition day, with much\ncooler air late week (upper 30s to mid 40s).",
